      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "At higher pH levels (closer to 7.0), the hydrogen ion concentration drops, reducing the positive charge needed to balance the negative oxalate or phosphate ions that bind iron and manganese. Without sufficient acidity, these micronutrients react with soil proteins and minerals to form stable, insoluble precipitates like ferrihydrite or manganese oxide, effectively locking them out of the soil solution. This chemical locking explains why simply adding more fertilizer often fails; the excess nutrients cannot dissolve to be absorbed even if they are physically present in the root zone.",
